_sync_firewall_note() {
    # LAN sync is inbound: a paired device connects to port 47823 on every copy
    # it makes. Omarchy (and Ubuntu) ship ufw on by default, which drops that
    # silently -- sync then looks healthy in both directions and delivers in
    # one. We only say so; opening a port is the admin's call, not a package's.
    systemctl is-active --quiet ufw 2>/dev/null || return 0
    grep -qs -- '--dport 47823' /etc/ufw/user.rules /etc/ufw/user6.rules && return 0
    cat <<'EOF'
ufw is active and inbound TCP 47823 is closed. LAN sync needs it -- without it
clips from your other devices are dropped before Clippy sees them (your own
copies still reach them, which is what makes this one hard to spot). If you use
sync, allow it on your LAN, e.g.:

  sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 47823 proto tcp comment 'Clippy LAN sync'

EOF
}

post_install() {
    cat <<'EOF'

Clippy is installed. To finish setting it up:

  clippy daemon &            # start it now (autostarts after the first launch)
  clippy setup-shortcut      # how to bind a key on your desktop

On Omarchy/Hyprland, Settings → shortcut picker writes the binding for you
into ~/.config/hypr/bindings.lua. Check the key is free first with
`omarchy menu keybindings --print` — SUPER+V and SUPER+CTRL+V are taken.

EOF
    _sync_firewall_note
}

post_upgrade() {
    # The running daemon keeps the old code until it is restarted. Say so
    # rather than killing it -- a silent kill would stop capturing clips.
    echo "Clippy updated. Restart the daemon to pick it up:  clippy quit && clippy daemon &"
    _sync_firewall_note
}
